Starting at the bottom-left gives the opposite pair of moves from the top-right version. Moving right reaches larger values in the row, and moving up reaches smaller values in the column.

If the current value is smaller than the target, move right. If it is larger, move up. For a one-cell matrix, the loop simply checks that cell and stops. Empty input is handled before reading the column count.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean searchMatrix(int[][] matrix, int target) {
        if (matrix == null || matrix.length == 0 || matrix[0].length == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        int row = matrix.length - 1;
        int col = 0;

        while (row >= 0 && col < matrix[0].length) {
            int current = matrix[row][col];

            if (current == target) {
                return true;
            }
            if (current > target) {
                --row;
            } else {
                ++col;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows + columns)`, since each move removes a row or a column.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The top-right corner gives a simple way to remove one row or column at each step. Values become smaller when moving left and larger when moving down. So a value that is too large means moving left, while a value that is too small means moving down.

The matrix is only read, not changed. The empty matrix check also avoids accessing `matrix[0]` when there are no rows.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
 bool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>> &matrix, int target){
        if (matrix.empty() || matrix[0].empty()) {
            return false;
        }

        int row = 0;
        int col = static_cast<int>(matrix[0].size()) - 1;

        while (row < static_cast<int>(matrix.size()) && col >= 0) {
            int current = matrix[row][col];

            if (current == target) {
                return true;
            }
            if (current > target) {
                --col;
            } else {
                ++row;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(rows + columns)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version splits the remaining rectangle around its middle cell. When the middle value is too small, the top-left part can be ignored. When it is too large, the bottom-right part can be ignored. The other three smaller rectangles are searched recursively.

A dry run with target `5` first checks the center of the usual example matrix. The target is smaller than that value, so the bottom-right region is skipped, and the remaining regions are checked.

This is more complicated and usually slower than staircase search, but it shows another way to use both sorting rules. The input is only read.

```go
func searchMatrix(matrix [][]int, target int) bool {
    if len(matrix) == 0 || len(matrix[0]) == 0 {
        return false
    }

    var search func(int, int, int, int) bool
    search = func(top, bottom, left, right int) bool {
        if top > bottom || left > right {
            return false
        }

        if target < matrix[top][left] || target > matrix[bottom][right] {
            return false
        }

        middleRow := top + (bottom-top)/2
        middleCol := left + (right-left)/2
        middleValue := matrix[middleRow][middleCol]

        if middleValue == target {
            return true
        }

        if target < middleValue {
            return search(top, middleRow-1, left, middleCol-1) ||
                search(top, middleRow-1, middleCol, right) ||
                search(middleRow, bottom, left, middleCol-1)
        }

        return search(middleRow+1, bottom, middleCol+1, right) ||
            search(top, middleRow, middleCol+1, right) ||
            search(middleRow+1, bottom, left, middleCol)
    }

    return search(0, len(matrix)-1, 0, len(matrix[0])-1)
}
```

For a square matrix, the worst-case time is about `O(3^log2(n))`, or `O(n^log2(3))`. The recursion uses `O(log n)` stack space for a square matrix.

Each row is sorted, so binary search can be used instead of checking every value in that row. The code repeats this for all rows and returns as soon as one search succeeds.

For example, if the target is outside the first and last value of a row, that row is skipped. This small check is not required for correctness, but it saves some binary searches.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean searchMatrix(int[][] matrix, int target) {
        if (matrix == null || matrix.length == 0 || matrix[0].length == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int[] row : matrix) {
            if (target < row[0] || target > row[row.length - 1]) {
                continue;
            }

            int left = 0;
            int right = row.length - 1;

            while (left <= right) {
                int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

                if (row[middle] == target) {
                    return true;
                }
                if (row[middle] < target) {
                    left = middle + 1;
                } else {
                    right = middle - 1;
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * log(columns))` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

